          "case_name": "Matter of Hofbauer",
          "citation": "47 N.Y.2d 648, 393 N.E.2d 1009, 419 N.Y.S.2d 936 (1979)",
          "court": "New York Court of Appeals",
          "year": 1979,
          "date_decided": "1979-07-10",
          "holding": "The Court affirmed dismissal of neglect petitions, holding that parents who sought accredited medical assistance and chose a treatment recommended by a duly licensed physician and not totally rejected by all responsible medical authority have not failed to supply adequate medical care.",
          "relevance": "This is the leading New York authority on when the State may override parental medical decision-making for a child.",
          "quote": "have provided for their child a treatment which is recommended by their physician and which has not been totally rejected by all responsible medical authority",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/5683552/in-re-hofbauer/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 5683552
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Matter of Storar (Eichner v. Dillon)",
          "citation": "52 N.Y.2d 363, 420 N.E.2d 64, 438 N.Y.S.2d 266 (1981)",
          "court": "New York Court of Appeals",
          "year": 1981,
          "date_decided": "1981-03-31",
          "holding": "Reversing in the Storar case, the Court held that although a parent or guardian may consent to medical treatment on behalf of an infant, the parent may not deny a child all treatment for a life-threatening condition even on religious grounds, and that the mother of a profoundly retarded man with the mental age of an infant therefore could not refuse the blood transfusions needed to keep him alive.",
          "relevance": "This is the Court of Appeals' statement of when the State may authorize immediate lifesaving medical care over a parent's objection, and it marks the boundary with Matter of Hofbauer: courts do not choose among reasonable treatments the parents have selected, but they will not allow a parent to withhold all treatment where the child's life is at stake.",
          "quote": "the courts may not permit a parent to deny a child all treatment for a condition which threatens his life",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/5684709/eichner-v-dillon/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 5684709
        }
